Lp -- version 1.1C
------------------

Lp is a print spooler for the Series 3, inspired by the Unix
utility of the same name.

Lp presides over a directory \TMP on the S3, where various
applications can leave plain text files. These file should have a
.PRN extension; if so Lp will recognise them and the file names
will be listed below the Lp icon.

At any time a user can choose to print one of the files by simply
keying ENTER on the filename. Lp will then send the file to the
printer device currently selected. On completion the program
offers the option to delete the file printed.


Installing Lp
-------------

Copy the LP.APP file to the \APP directory of the Series 3
internal drive. Create a new first-level subdirectory called \TMP
on the internal drive. Use the Install application (Psion-I)
option and pick LP.APP, whereupon the Lp icon will appear on the
shell display.


Using Lp
--------

Lp uses the printer settings established by the user in the
Printer Setup (Psion-P) dialog. When Lp is first started, it
displays the standard print control dialog, so that margins, page
length etc. may be customised by the user.

Any errors that arise during printing are communicated to the
user by means of a standard notifier box. On completion of
printing the user is prompted as to whether or not the file just
printed should be deleted. The default (keying ENTER) is to
delete the file; keying ESCAPE retains the file.

Lp can be terminated by quitting it from the shell, using the
Delete key when the 'Lp' name is highlighted. When Lp is next
started, it will present the print control dialog again.

For test purposes the file TEST.PRN can be used, once copied to
the \TMP directory. TEST.PRN just exercises all the print
controls recognised currently by Lp (see below).

It is possible to print any text file using Lp - not just those
in a \TMP directory. To do so from the shell, position the
highlight beneath the Lp icon and key TAB. Navigate to the
desired file and key ENTER with the file name highlighted.

Print controls
--------------

Lp recognises the following print controls embedded in source
files. The print control lead-in character is a circumflex (^).

Code     Interpretation

^0       Cancel all enhancements
^1       Bold on
^2       Bold off
^3       Italics on
^4       Italics off
^5       Underscore on
^6       Underscore off
^7       Toggle superscript
^8       Toggle subscript
^9       Start a new page for following text

Any other two-character sequence starting with ^ is ignored (not
printed), except for '^^' which is printed as a single '^'.

Note that ^7 and ^8 controls are toggles, to be used in pairs,
for example:

  Water, chemical name H^82^8O

Any attributes set at the end of printing one file are not
carried over to the next to be printed. Each file printed starts
with all print controls disabled.

The maximum line length in a file to be printed is 255
characters.


Note for OPL application developers
-----------------------------------

Here is a typical section of code that creates a print file for
use by Lp.

  #define TEMP_DIRECTORY "loc::m:\tmp\"

  REM Ensure temporary directory exists
  trap mkdir TEMP_DIRECTORY

  REM Get a temporary file name
  filenam$=prnname$:(TEMP_DIRECTORY,"Super")
  trap lopen TEMP_DIRECTORY+filenam$
  REM Out of space is the only likely error
  if err :raise err :endif

  REM Output then close
  lprint "^1Super-Duper Version 69^2"
  REM etc.
  lclose
  REM Announce the file created
  giprint "Created "+filenam$

Where the auxiliary prnname$ function is along the following
lines:

  proc prnname$:(loc$,app$)
  local s$(5), i%

  REM Make a filename prefix from first 5 letters
  s$=left$(app$,5)
  i%=1
  while i%<999  REM Sanity check!
    if not exist(loc$+s$+gen$(i%,5)+".prn")
      break
    endif
    i%=i%+1
  endwh
  return s$+gen$(i%,5)+".prn"
  endp

The ^n print controls can be defined as macros or placed in
string variables, of course.


